// Why an input is not canonical RLP — seven rules, each with the offset it broke at.
//
// The original answers a `string`, built as it goes with `itoa` from `@/packages/fmt`:
//
//     c.error = "a length prefix with a leading zero is not canonical";
//
// Two things are wrong with that and only one of them is the usual one.
//
// ## 1. The usual one: a sentence cannot be matched
//
// Seven distinct rules, one type. `packages/rlp/README.md` says the tests need them apart —
// *"'one of twenty-six was accepted' is a bisect, `wrongSizeList was accepted` is a fix"* — and gets
// it by naming the *fixture*, because the decoder's answer cannot say which rule fired. A consensus
// client that wants to treat *ran off the end* differently from *not canonical* has the same
// problem, and these are genuinely different: the first can be a truncated network read and the
// second is a peer sending something no honest encoder produces.
//
// ## 2. And none of the messages says *where*
//
// This is the half that is specific to this package. Every message names the rule and none of them
// names the offset:
//
//     "a length prefix with a leading zero is not canonical"
//
// In a 500-byte trie node with sixteen children that is a sentence and a search. The cursor knows —
// `c.at` is right there when the error is set — and the string does not carry it. Every member below
// takes `at`, and it costs nothing, because the position is already in the hand that writes the
// fault.
//
// Worth separating from finding 1 because they have different fixes and only one of them needs a
// language feature: the string could always have had the offset in it.

/** The declared length ran past the end of the input. Not necessarily malice — a short read does it. */
export struct Truncated { i32 at; i32 want; i32 have; }

/** `0x81 0x05`: a single byte below `0x80` is itself, never a one-byte string. */
export struct NotSelfEncoded { i32 at; u8 byte; }

/** `0xb8 0x00`: a length prefix carries no leading zero, however plausible it looks. */
export struct LeadingZero { i32 at; }

/** `0xb8 0x02` where `0x82` would do: the long form is for lengths above 55. */
export struct NotMinimal { i32 at; i32 len; }

/** A four-byte prefix can spell 4 GB and an `i32` cannot hold it. See below. */
export struct TooLong { i32 at; u64 len; }

/** A member ran past the list's own declared end: the list says one thing and its contents another. */
export struct ListOverrun { i32 at; }

/** A complete item, and then more bytes — so the input has two readings. */
export struct Trailing { i32 at; i32 extra; }

/**
 * Anything `decode` refuses.
 *
 * **Two groups, and a caller may want to tell them apart.** `Truncated` is *the input is not all
 * here*; the other six are *this is not canonical RLP and no honest encoder produced it*. The same
 * split `@/packages/gzip`'s `Fault` makes between `SourceFailed` and `Corrupt`, and for the same
 * reason: one of them is worth retrying with more bytes and the rest are not. Not written as a
 * nested union here, because unlike gzip's there is exactly one member on the retryable side and a
 * union of one is a rename.
 */
export union<Truncated, NotSelfEncoded, LeadingZero, NotMinimal, TooLong, ListOverrun, Trailing>
  RlpFault;

// **Flat, and that is now a choice rather than the only option.** Every member here is disjoint, so
// nesting and flattening agree and this declaration says nothing about which the language means.
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has the two consumers that disagree — `@/packages/box`'s gunzip needs
// nesting for `Err(is Corrupt)` and its `upper.wac` needs flattening so a stacked transform's
// `NotText` is one member rather than two depths. This union is evidence for neither, which is why
// it is worth saying: most unions are like this one, and the decision is made by the two that
// are not.

// ## `TooLong` is a refusal the language forces and the format does not
//
// The original is explicit that this is a decision:
//
// > A length that does not fit an `i32` is refused rather than wrapped. `packages/ssz` has the same
// > case and gives the same answer: a four-byte length prefix can spell 4 GB, an `i32` cannot hold
// > it, and a wrapped negative would be read as a short item. There is no input this repo can hold
// > for which the refusal is wrong.
//
// Right, and the last sentence is the whole of the justification: it is fine **because nothing here
// has 4 GB of memory**. RLP itself permits an eight-byte length prefix, so the format's ceiling is
// 2^64 and wac's array index is an `i32`.
//
// So the member carries a `u64` — the length the input actually declared — rather than the `i32` it
// could not fit. A caller that wants to say *this input declares 6 GB and I cannot hold it* can;
// with the shipped `string` it says the same sentence for 2^31 and for 2^63.
//
// The language question underneath is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s and is not this package's to answer:
// **an array's length is an `i32` and a wire format's is not.** The original names the second
// package with the same case itself — `packages/ssz` — so two implementations in this repo refuse
// input their format permits, for a reason that is about the index type rather than about either
// format.
